2026-09-05 --:--  [v3-periphery]  burning liquidity takes two calls and cannot pay out directly.
    Expected decreaseLiquidity to return the tokens. It does not -- it credits tokensOwed on the
    position, and a second collect() call moves them, so every unwind is two calls to the position
    manager even when you want the whole thing in one place. v4 does the equivalent inside a single
    unlock() with one netted delta, which is exactly the comparison this project exists to measure,
    so this is a data point rather than a complaint. Noting it because the two-step is also what
    makes a *partial* unwind awkward: you size the burn, then collect sweeps everything owed
    including accrued fees, so the amount that arrives is not the amount you sized for.
    (cost: ~15m reasoning about what collect actually returns)

2026-09-07 --:--  [v3-core / testing]  a concentrated pool has no "slightly dislocated" state.
    Wanted a fork test where the route venue is priced a little away from the park venue, to drive
    a sourcing estimate a few tens of bp short. There is no such state to reach on Base's stable
    pools. USDC/USDT 0.01% holds 94k USDC against L = 3.9e14, so its entire token0 side lives
    inside ~5 ticks; the 0.05% pool holds 4.9k against L = 7.6e12, ~13 ticks. Pushing to a named
    price -- the obvious way to write it, sqrtPriceLimitX96 at +1% -- walks straight through the
    band and leaves the pool with literally nothing on the far side, so the residual sold for
    5.87 USDC instead of 2,507. Price impact against a concentrated pool is not a curve you can
    aim at, it is a cliff: ~0bp, then ~100%.
    The fix was to stop naming a price and name an amount instead, then sweep amounts until the
    window appeared. It is wide once found (2.5k-4.5k of USDT all land in it) but nothing in the
    pool's public surface tells you where it is -- liquidity() is the active-tick L, which says
    nothing about how far that L extends, and there is no view for "how much token0 is left before
    the book thins". Tick bitmap walking is the only answer and that is D6 work.
    Worth saying plainly because it cuts both ways for this project: the same cliff is why the
    escalation ceiling has to be a bounded multiple rather than a retry loop.
    (cost: ~40m, most of it reading a trace to find out the pool had been emptied)

2026-09-07 --:--  [v4-periphery]  minting a v4 position fails with an opaque selector from a third contract.
    Approved USDC and USDT to the PositionManager, encoded MINT_POSITION + SETTLE_PAIR, called
    modifyLiquidities. Reverted with `custom error 0xd81b2f2e:` and nothing else -- no name, no
    args, and the selector belongs to neither the position manager nor the pool manager. Only a
    -vvvv trace shows the last frame is 0x000000000022D473030F116dDEE9F6B43aC78BA3, i.e. Permit2,
    and 0xd81b2f2e is AllowanceExpired(uint256).
    The actual requirement is that v4's PositionManager pays via Permit2, so the approval is two
    steps -- ERC20.approve(PERMIT2) then PERMIT2.approve(token, positionManager, amount, expiry) --
    and an expiration of 0 from a never-used Permit2 allowance reads as *expired* rather than as
    *absent*. A plain ERC20 approval to the position manager, which is what v3 needs and therefore
    what anyone arriving from v3 will write, silently does nothing.
    Two separate costs. Naming the error would have saved most of it: `AllowanceExpired` in a v4
    mint trace points straight at Permit2. And a docs line at the top of the v4 minting guide
    saying "payment goes through Permit2, here are the two approvals" would have saved all of it.
    Notable that the whole action encoding was correct first time -- the failure was entirely in
    the payment path, which is the part that differs from v3 without saying so.
    (cost: ~25m)

2026-09-08 10:40  [v4-core]  Expected `Currency` to support `!=` because it supports `==` ->
    `Currency` is a user-defined value type with an `==` operator attached but no `!=`, so
    `residualCurrency != ROUTE_CURRENCY0` fails to compile with "Built-in binary operator !=
    cannot be applied to types Currency and Currency". Have to write `!(a == b)`. Solidity has
    supported `using {f as !=}` since 0.8.19, so this is a one-line omission in `Currency.sol`
    rather than a language limit. Trivial to work around, but it is the kind of thing that reads
    as "I have the wrong import" for a minute before it reads as "the operator is missing".
    (cost: ~3m)

2026-09-08 15:10  [v4-core]  the tick-bitmap search is unusable from outside the pool.
    Expected to reuse `TickBitmap.nextInitializedTickWithinOneWord` for the multi-tick walk. Its
    first parameter is `mapping(int16 => uint256) storage self`, i.e. the pool's own storage, so it
    is callable only from inside a pool. Every integrator quoting a swap off-chain is reading the
    bitmap through `tickBitmap(wordPos)` (v3) or `StateLibrary.getTickBitmap` (v4) and therefore has
    a word *by value* — the one form the library does not accept. Re-implemented the ~20 lines of
    mask-and-`BitMath` arithmetic against a `uint256` argument.
    The split is one line of work: a `pure` overload taking `uint256 word` with the storage version
    calling it. As it stands the exact search a quoter must match bit-for-bit is the piece it is
    forced to copy by hand, and the two formulations differ between v3 (`(1 << bitPos) - 1 + (1 <<
    bitPos)`) and v4 (`word << (255 - bitPos)`), so "copy the one from the repo" is already
    ambiguous. Note this is precisely the case the official quoter contracts sidestep by simulating
    a real swap and reverting -- which is not available to a `view` that has to bisect.
    (cost: ~20m)

2026-09-08 15:35  [v4-core]  `computeSwapStep`'s four return values do not fit a swap loop.
    Expected to call `SwapMath.computeSwapStep` in a loop the way `Pool.swap` does. Stack too deep,
    at 0.8.34 without `--via-ir`: six arguments plus a four-value destructuring inside a `for` is
    over the limit. v4-core's own `Pool.sol` does not hit this because it holds the same state in a
    `SwapResult`/`StepComputations` struct, which reads as house style until you need the function
    on its own. Ended up doing the same thing — a `RouteSwap` memory struct — after trying block
    scoping and dropping a parameter first.
    Fixable in the library rather than in every caller: the four values are always consumed
    together, so returning a struct would make the function composable instead of leaving each
    integrator to rediscover the workaround. Turning on `--via-ir` is the other answer and costs a
    second compiler configuration this project has deliberately stayed free of.
    (cost: ~15m)

2026-09-08 16:20  [tooling]  quoting by tick walk multiplies archive reads, and CI noticed first.
    Expected the multi-tick walk to be free in a `view` -- it runs over `eth_call`, so gas is not
    the constraint. It is not free against a *forked* node: each bound now touches up to 128 bitmap
    words plus one `ticks()` per initialized tick, all distinct cold storage slots, where the
    single step touched `slot0` and `liquidity`. Two concurrent CI jobs on the same commit (the
    `push` and `pull_request` triggers both firing for a branch with an open PR) put that over the
    public Base endpoint's rate limit: HTTP 429 mid-suite, ten tests failing with `EVM error;
    database error: failed to get storage`, while the identical concurrent job passed.
    Two things worth carrying into FEEDBACK.md. The failure does not name a rate limit anywhere
    near the top -- it surfaces as a storage read failing on a pool address, which reads as a
    contract bug for a minute. And the general point for anyone quoting Uniswap off-chain: the
    honest bound is the one that reads the book, and reading the book is what makes a quoter
    RPC-expensive rather than gas-expensive. An official `view` quoter that walks ticks without
    simulating-and-reverting would be the thing that makes this cheap for everyone.
    (cost: ~10m, all of it diagnosis)

2026-09-10 11:05  [v3 / v4-core]  `slot0` and a realised fill price are quoted in opposite
    directions, and getting it wrong manufactures exactly the bug you are hunting.
    Measuring what the callback's residual sale actually got, to compare against the maker's
    slippage budget. The realised price falls out of the swap as `amountOut / amountIn` — USDC per
    USDT here, i.e. token0 per token1. `slot0`'s `sqrtPriceX96` is token1 per token0. They are
    reciprocals, so a direct comparison is wrong by construction, and the failure is quiet: an
    honest unattacked fill reported a 22.67bp deviation against a 10bp budget instead of its real
    8.13bp. That is a plausible-looking budget breach in a test suite whose entire purpose is
    finding budget breaches, so it reads as a finding rather than as a unit error. It only came
    apart because the attacked number, 61.34%, matched the independently-measured 61.86% loss and
    the honest one matched nothing.
    Not a bug in Uniswap — the orientation is documented and consistent. The friction is that
    nothing in the type system distinguishes the two, both are `uint256` WADs near 1e18 for a
    stable pair, and the sign of the error is small for small moves, so it survives a smoke test.
    A `sqrtPriceX96 -> price` helper in the periphery libraries taking the desired orientation as
    an argument would be the fix; every integrator writes this by hand today. Ended up routing both
    venues through a single `_priceOfResidual` so the inversion cannot be applied to one side of a
    comparison and not the other.
    (cost: ~25m)

2026-09-10 11:30  [v3]  there is no way to ask a pool what price a swap just executed at.
    Wanted the blended realised price over a settlement that hits the route venue twice (a sized
    burn plus an escalation). The `Swap` event carries per-swap amounts, so it means decoding two
    events and summing them; `slot0` after the fact gives the *ending* price, not the average, and
    for a swap that walks several ticks those differ by the whole point of the measurement.
    Settled on reading the pool's ERC-20 balances before and after the take and dividing, which is
    exact and aggregates naturally, but only works because nothing else moves that pool's balances
    inside the transaction — true here since park and route are different pools, and quietly false
    for any maker who routes through the venue they park in.
    The general shape: v3 tells you where the price *is* and what each swap *did*, but a callback
    or a guard reasoning about execution quality wants the average price of its own fill, and has
    to reconstruct it. Same gap the `minOut` pattern papers over off-chain.
    (cost: ~10m)

2026-09-10 12:15  [tooling]  the D6 rate-limit fix was only half of it, and the throttle knobs
    are not where the config lives.
    D6 stopped CI racing itself (`push` and `pull_request` both firing for a branch with an open
    PR). D7 hit HTTP 429 again on a *single* job: adding four fork tests that each snapshot,
    take, and walk the route book pushed one run's distinct cold storage reads over the public
    Base endpoint on its own. Same symptom as before — `failed to get storage for 0xD56d…` — and
    still nothing near the top of the message names a rate limit. Worth noting the shape for
    FEEDBACK.md: the honest bound is the one that reads the book, reading the book is what makes a
    quoter RPC-expensive, and the cost lands on *anyone forking to test an integration*, not just
    on the quoter's own users.
    Then the mitigation turned out not to exist, in three layers. `compute_units_per_second` and
    `fork_retry_backoff` under `[profile.ci]` emit "Found unknown config" and are ignored (forge
    1.5.1) — and only `forge config` prints that warning, `forge test` accepts the file silently
    and runs unthrottled. As CLI flags they refuse to parse without an accompanying `--rpc-url`,
    which this suite has no use for because it forks from inside `setUp()`. And with `--rpc-url`
    supplied to satisfy the parser, they still do nothing: **`--compute-units-per-second 1`
    against a cleared cache finishes a fork test in 2.44s**, so the throttle plainly does not
    reach forks created by `vm.createSelectFork`. Three ways to believe a suite is rate-limited
    when it is not, and the only one that says anything out loud says it in a different command.
    The knob a cheatcode-forking suite actually needs — throttle *this* fork — has no spelling.
    (cost: ~45m)

2026-09-10 12:50  [tooling]  `actions/cache` will not save from a failing job, which is the one
    whose fetches are worth keeping.
    The combined `actions/cache@v4` only writes its entry in the post step when the job
    *succeeded*. For a fork suite priming an RPC cache that is exactly backwards: the run that
    dies partway through fetching new state is the run that fetched the most new state. D7 hit the
    deadlock — the suite had grown past what the public endpoint serves in one run, every attempt
    429'd partway, none of them saved, and each retry restarted from the same stale cache. A green
    suite locally, a permanently red CI, and re-running never converges.
    Split into `actions/cache/restore` plus `actions/cache/save` with `if: always()` and
    consecutive runs accumulate instead. Worth knowing before you conclude the endpoint is simply
    too small: it may be that nothing you fetch is ever being kept. `save-always` used to express
    this on the combined action and was deprecated without the docs foregrounding the split as its
    replacement for this case.
    (cost: ~20m)

2026-09-10 15:20  [v3-periphery]  the canonical TWAP helper is unreachable from 0.8, so every
    integrator re-implements its rounding — and the rounding is the part that is easy to get wrong.
    Needed an arithmetic-mean tick over a window: `observe([window, 0])`, difference the
    cumulatives, divide. `OracleLibrary.consult` in v3-periphery does exactly this and is the
    reference implementation, but v3-periphery is pinned to `=0.7.6` and cannot be compiled in a
    profile that follows Midnight at 0.8.34. So it gets copied by hand, which is fine until you
    notice the one line that is not arithmetic: Solidity's `/` truncates toward zero, and the mean
    tick has to round *down*, so a negative cumulative delta that does not divide evenly needs an
    explicit `--`. Omit it and the reference is biased in one direction only — a systematic tilt on
    any pair whose price sits just below tick zero, which is most stable pairs quoted one way
    round, and invisible on any pair quoted the other.
    Same shape as the `TickBitmap` note from D6: the piece an integrator must match bit-for-bit is
    the piece the libraries make hardest to reuse. A `pure` mean-tick helper taking the two
    cumulatives and the window would be ~6 lines, would compile on any 0.8, and would remove a
    silent-bias footgun from every 0.8 integration. Tested here against a mock pool with chosen
    cumulatives, because a real pool at a pinned block cannot be made to produce a negative
    non-dividing delta on demand.
    (cost: ~20m)

2026-09-10 15:45  [v3]  `observe()` fails as a bare string, at the worst possible moment.
    A pool whose `observationCardinality` cannot reach back far enough reverts `OLD` — a plain
    `require` string, no error type, no mention of what window it could have served. For a price
    reference that is the difference between a deployment that fails and an *offer* that fails: the
    oracle is not consulted until settlement, so a badly configured reference deploys fine, quotes
    fine (`buyerAssetsBound` reverting reads as "no bound available" to a routing layer), and then
    reverts every take on the taker's gas. Wrapped the probe in the constructor and restated it as
    `InsufficientObservationHistory(pool, window)` so it fails at the only moment anyone can act.
    Cardinality only ever grows, so the constructor check is sound rather than merely early. The
    friction is that this is entirely on the integrator to think of — nothing in the interface hints
    that the oracle is the one dependency whose readiness is a deployment-time property.
    (cost: ~10m)

2026-09-10 17:20  [v4]  The truncated-oracle example has been deleted from `v4-periphery`.
    `PLAN.md` schedules a `TruncatedOracleRef` for D10 on the strength of Uniswap shipping a
    reference truncated-tick oracle hook. It is not there any more: `src/hooks/` in `v4-periphery`
    `main` contains exactly one entry (`permissionedPools`), and `src/libraries/` has no oracle at
    all. A GitHub code search for `TruncatedOracle` returns twenty-odd forks in third-party repos
    and nothing in a Uniswap-owned one.
    That matters more for a hook than for an ordinary deleted example, because a hook is the one
    kind of contract an integrator cannot simply point at — reading a truncated oracle in a fork
    test means *deploying* one, which means having its source and knowing its licence. The
    surviving copies are MIT, but they are unattributed forks of a deleted file, several of them
    edited, and there is no upstream to diff against. The maintained thing that does exist is
    OpenZeppelin's `uniswap-hooks` (MIT), which is what D10 will use instead.
    An example that gets deleted rather than archived leaves its downstream with no way to tell a
    faithful copy from a modified one. A tag, a deprecation note, or an `examples/` repo would each
    have been enough.
    (cost: ~25m, and it moved D10's source of record)

2026-09-10 17:35  [v4]  BUSL-1.1 and MIT live in the same tree with no manifest.
    `lib/v4-core/src` mixes `PoolManager.sol` (BUSL-1.1) with the libraries and interfaces around it
    (MIT), and `lib/midnight/src` does the same with its core and its periphery. Neither ships a
    machine-readable statement of which is which, so "we only bind the licensed-restrictively parts
    through interfaces" — the claim that lets this repo be GPL-2.0-or-later at all — can only be
    checked by opening every file an import resolves to.
    And the mixing is not shallow. `StateLibrary` and `TransientStateLibrary` are MIT, and both
    import BUSL-1.1 files — `Position`, `Lock`, `CurrencyReserves`, `NonzeroDeltaCount` — so reading
    a v4 pool's state through the library Uniswap provides for it pulls BUSL into the build whether
    or not the integrator ever names a BUSL file. Nothing is wrong with that, since BUSL-1.1 grants
    redistribution and non-production use outright, but an integrator who needs to know their BUSL
    surface (anyone shipping under a copyleft licence, which is anyone forking Morpho's periphery)
    cannot learn it by reading their own imports. I got this wrong first time round and had to
    rewrite the check against Foundry's build-info to get a true answer: 29 direct imports, 64
    sources in the actual closure, four of them BUSL.
    A `LICENSES.md` at the root of each repo, or simply a note on `StateLibrary` saying what it
    pulls in, would make this a lookup instead of a crawl.
    The per-file headers are correct and complete — they are just not aggregated anywhere, and the
    one place aggregation would matter most is the transitive case.
    (cost: ~20m, plus ~30m rewriting it after the one-hop version gave a confidently wrong answer)

2026-09-10 19:10  [v4]  A mean-tick TWAP is quantised to a basis point, and nothing says so.
    Building a reference-relative slippage guard, every honest v4 fill failed a 1bp budget. The
    cause was not impact: the v3 pool the reference reads and the v4 pool the residual sells into
    are *both at tick 7*, and are still 0.75bp apart on price. A tick is a basis point wide, so a
    mean-tick reference lands on the tick boundary while a venue's spot sits somewhere inside it.
    Two venues "at the same tick" therefore agree only to within one tick, and an integrator
    comparing a TWAP against an execution price is charging that quantisation as slippage.
    The consequence is a floor on the smallest budget anyone can enforce against an `observe()`-
    derived reference, and it is nowhere in the docs. `OracleLibrary.consult` returns a tick;
    `getQuoteAtTick` converts it; neither mentions that the answer is only tick-accurate, and both
    read as if they were giving you a price. The fix is a sentence, not code: say that a mean tick
    is accurate to one tick spacing of the *oracle* pool and that budgets below that are not
    meaningful.
    (cost: ~50m, most of it spent looking for a bug in my own port)

2026-09-10 21:40  [midnight]  `take` looks unsigned, and offers are signed somewhere else.
    `take(Offer offer, bytes ratifierData, uint256 units, ...)` has no signature parameter. Reading
    the interface, the honest conclusion is that a Midnight offer is an unsigned struct and the
    maker's consent must live entirely in on-chain authorisation — which is what I concluded, and it
    is wrong. Offers are signed; the signature travels inside `ratifierData` and is verified by the
    ratifier the offer names, and Morpho operates a canonical `EcrecoverRatifier` on Base for
    exactly this.
    Nothing in `IMidnight` points at that. `ratifierData` is `bytes`, the natspec on `take` does not
    say what belongs in it, and `IRatifier.isRatified` is generic by design. The information exists
    — in the `midnight-pocs` repo and the SDK — but the interface an integrator reads first actively
    suggests the opposite, and "this protocol has no offer signatures" is a conclusion that changes
    how you design a whole integration.
    One line on `take` naming `EcrecoverRatifier` as the standard scheme, or a `@dev` pointing at
    `src/ratifiers/`, would close it. The design underneath is good: signing a Merkle root means one
    signature authorises a whole book and `cancelRoot` retires it in one transaction.
    (cost: ~40m, and a bespoke ratifier contract written for a problem that did not exist)

2026-09-10 21:55  [midnight]  Constructing an offer hash pulls in a compiler requirement.
    This project binds deployed Midnight through interfaces only, on one compiler profile with no IR
    pipeline — which works for every interface and library it imports, right up to `HashLib`. That
    is the first Midnight source it *compiles* rather than binds, and it does not fit in the stack
    without `via_ir`: Midnight's own `foundry.toml` sets it, ours deliberately does not.
    So an integrator's compiler settings are free of Midnight's exactly until they need to construct
    or verify an offer, and then they are not. That is a surprising place for a build dependency to
    surface, and nothing signposts it — `HashLib` looks like an ordinary internal library.
    Worked around with a path-scoped `compilation_restrictions` entry so the rest of the build keeps
    its single profile. A note in the ratifier docs saying "these need via_ir" would have saved the
    diagnosis; shipping a precomputed-digest helper, or a view function on the ratifier that returns
    the digest for an offer, would remove the need to compile it at all.
    (cost: ~25m)
